"best","good","good","best","best","good","good","inaccuracy","best","good","best","best","good","best","best","best","good","inaccuracy","inaccuracy","good","best","inaccuracy","good","best","inaccuracy","good","inaccuracy","good","best","excellent","best","mistake","great","excellent","best","inaccuracy","miss","inaccuracy","brilliant","best","best"],"moments":[{"ply":73,"name":"37.Rxa6","cls":"mistake","color":"w","headline":"37.Rxa6 is a mistake: White's winning chances go from 44% to 32%.","points":["Black answers 37…Re2: the rook reaches the seventh rank, where 3 pawns sit.","After Re2, Black wins 2 points of material within 6 moves.","Better was 37.Rc4 (−0.66), which attacks the c7 pawn, which is undefended."],"better":"37.Rc4 attacks the c7 pawn, which is undefended, and shores up the c2 pawn. Engine line: 37…Re2 38.Qf1 Nf6 39.Rd1 d5 40.Rc3 (−0.66)."},{"ply":74,"name":"37…Re2","cls":"great","color":"b","headline":"37…Re2! The only good move here: every alternative is clearly worse.","points":["The rook reaches the seventh rank, where 3 pawns sit.","Adds a decisive attacker on the g2 pawn: 2 attackers against 1 defender.","The next best move, 37…Nb6, would drop Black's winning chances from 68% to 54% (−0.40).","37…Re2 wins two pawns, picking it up with 38…Rxc2.","Sets up a sequence and wins two pawns once the captures settle, picking it up with 38…Rxc2.","White answers 38.Qg1."]},{"ply":78,"name":"39…Qe4","cls":"miss","color":"b","headline":"39…Qe4 lets the win slip: 39…Qxb2 kept Black winning. Black's winning chances go from 79% to 61%.","points":["White answers 40.Bg3: moving away uncovers the queen on f1, which now hits the f7 pawn.","Better was 39…Qxb2 (−3.65), which takes the pawn on b2."],"better":"39…Qxb2 wins two pawns and trades off the attacked queen instead of losing it to Rxc2. After 40.Bg3 Rxg2+ 41.Qxg2 Qxc1 42.Rc6 Qe3 43.Rxc7 Ne5, Black comes out a pawn up (−3.65)."},{"ply":80,"name":"40…Rxg2+","cls":"brilliant","color":"b","headline":"40…Rxg2+!! A real sacrifice: Black gives up material and the engine still rates it best.","points":["Puts the rook on g2 where it can be taken.","White can take, but after 41.Qxg2 Qxf4+ 42.Kh1 Re1+ Black mates in 6.","Takes the pawn on g2.","The rook forks the king and the pawn: both can't be saved at once.","40…Rxg2+ forks the king and the pawn.","The rook joins the attack: it now hits 3 squares around the White king."],"why":[{"title":"The idea","text":"40…Rxg2+!! offers the rook on g2. Taking it leads to mate."},{"title":"What Black gives up","text":"40…Rxg2+ puts the rook on g2 where it can be taken by the queen on f1. That's a whole rook on offer."},{"title":"If White takes the rook","text":"41.Qxg2 is met by 41…Qxf4+, taking the bishop on f4 with check. Then 42…Re1+ gives check, and 43…Qf3+ gives check. Black forces mate in 6. This is White's best try."},{"title":"Without the sacrifice","text":"The best move without the sacrifice, 40…Ne5, is clearly weaker (−0.68 against #−6). White answers 41.Ra3, preparing Qf2 and Qc2. That would leave Black a little better."}]}],"opening":{"name":"Ruy Lopez: Closed, Anti-Marshall","eco":"C88","points":["Both sides followed opening theory up to 8…Na5: 16 moves in all, 8 by White and 8 by Black.","Theory runs out after 8…Na5: too few games between strong players reached this position to go further."]}}